When a small group of living creatures (a small population) is separated from the main population from which it originates (e.g. if it travels above a mountain range or river, or if it moves to a new island so it cannot easily go back), it often ends up in an environment different from the one in which it was before.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so the new population will need new characteristics or adaptations to be a powerful competitor compared to what it needed before.
The population of origin has not changed at all, it still needs the same adjustments.
Over the course of time, as the new population adapts to its new environment, it begins to look less and less like the other population.
Finally, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will seem so different that they can no longer be regarded as belonging to the same species.
We call this “speciation process,” which simply means the formation of new species; speciation is an inevitable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
