Technological determinism is a term that encompasses a wide range of ideas in practice, ranging from technological thrust or the technological imperative to a strict sense that the destiny of humanity is dictated by an underlying logic related to scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism agree on two general ideas: technological development itself unravels according to a process that goes far beyond cultural and political influences, and technology has an impact on conditioned societies intrinsically rather than socially.
For example, it could be said that the automobile necessarily leads to the development of the roads.
However, a national road network is not economically viable for a handful of cars, which is why new production methods are being developed to reduce the cost of owning a car.
The massive possession of cars also leads to a greater impact of accidents on the roads, which leads to the invention of new techniques in the field of health care for the treatment of personal injuries.
